14. Recommendations / Mitigation
Based on the vulnerabilities identified during the penetration testing assessment, the following mitigation strategies are recommended to improve the overall security posture of the system.
14.1 Prevent Reverse Tabnabbing Attacks
To mitigate reverse tabnabbing vulnerabilities, all external links that open in a new browser tab should include the following attributes:
rel="noopener"
rel="noreferrer"
This prevents the newly opened page from gaining access to the originating page via the window.opener property.
Example:
HTML
Copy code
<a href="https://example.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Link</a>
Additionally, user-submitted links should be validated and monitored to prevent malicious redirection attempts.
14.2 Strengthen Authentication Mechanisms
Weak or reused credentials increase the risk of unauthorized access.
Recommended controls include:
Enforcing strong password policies
Implementing account lockout mechanisms
Enabling multi-factor authentication (MFA)
Preventing password reuse across services
These measures significantly reduce the risk of brute-force and credential-stuffing attacks.
14.3 Secure File Permissions and Access Controls
Improper file permissions allowed attackers to modify critical scripts.
To mitigate this risk:
Restrict write permissions on sensitive files.
Ensure only authorized users can modify application scripts.
Regularly audit file permissions using commands such as:
Bash
Copy code
ls -la
Principle of Least Privilege should always be enforced.
14.4 Secure Scheduled Tasks
The scheduled Python script executed with elevated privileges allowed privilege escalation.
Recommended mitigation:
Ensure scheduled scripts are owned by root and not writable by other users.
Regularly audit cron jobs and scheduled tasks.
Store scripts in restricted directories.
Example command to check cron jobs:
Bash
Copy code
crontab -l
14.5 Implement Proper Access Controls
The system allowed certain users to run privileged commands without proper restrictions.
To prevent this:
Carefully configure sudo permissions.
Restrict commands in /etc/sudoers.
Regularly review sudo privileges using:
Bash
Copy code
sudo -l
14.6 Improve System Hardening
System hardening measures should be implemented to reduce the attack surface.
Recommended steps:
Keep all software and packages updated.
Disable unnecessary services and ports.
Apply regular security patches.
Implement intrusion detection systems (IDS).
14.7 Security Awareness and Monitoring
User awareness and system monitoring are essential for detecting malicious activity.
Recommended practices include:
Conducting regular security awareness training.
Implementing centralized logging and monitoring.
Monitoring suspicious login attempts and unusual activity.
